<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ellalan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 205–161 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Elara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ellalan, called Elara in the Pali chronicles, was a Tamil prince from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> country who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> for about 44 years in the 2nd century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a> remembers him as a just ruler, although he was not a Buddhist. He was defeated and killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutugemunu">Dutugemunu</a> of Ruhuna around 161 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ellalan.jpg" alt="Ellalan" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MadrasHighCourtManuNeedhiCholanStatue_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 237 BCE two adventurers from southern India took control of Anuradhapura, and in 145 BCE the Chola <b>Elara</b> took its throne, ruling for forty-four years until the Sinhalese Dutugamunu defeated him.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 161 BCE Dutugemunu defeated the Tamil ruler <b>Elara</b> and united the island.</blockquote><a href="/state/anuradhapura-kingdom">Anuradhapura Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Rice farming on the delta supported the kingdom, and Chola adventurers, such as <b>Elara</b>, at times ruled in Sri Lanka.</blockquote><a href="/state/early-cholas">Early Cholas</a></li><li><blockquote>Rugged and distant from the capital, it served for centuries as a refuge for princes and resistance against invaders; King Kavantissa ruled there, and his son Dutugemunu marched north to defeat <b>Elara</b> around 161 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ruhuna">Kingdom of Ruhuna</a></li></ul></section>
